.Dd August 13, 2026
.Dt LDAPSTNS.CONF 5
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm ldapstns.conf
.Nd configuration file for ldapstns
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
holds the settings of
.Xr ldapstns 8
itself.
Everything about the STNS API - where it is, how to authenticate to it, how
long to wait for it - is in
.Xr stns.conf 5
instead, which is read by
.Xr stns-key-wrapper 8
as well and can be copied unchanged from a machine running any other STNS
client.
.Pp
The file is TOML, as
.Xr stns.conf 5
is.
Every key has a working default and the file itself is optional; a key that is
present and not listed below is reported to
.Xr syslogd 8
and ignored, so a misspelling does not quietly leave the daemon serving
something nobody asked for.
.Pp
.Nm
is not a
.Xr launchd.plist 5 .
That file says how to start the program, which is a different question from
what the program should do, and macOS keeps the two apart as well.
.Sh SETTINGS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic listen No = Qq Ar address
.It Ic listen No = Ql ["address", "address"]
One address, or a list of them.
Either family; the default is both loopbacks,
.Qq 127.0.0.1
and
.Qq ::1 .
.Pp
Both, because on this system
.Qq localhost
resolves to
.Li ::1
before it resolves to
.Li 127.0.0.1 :
a daemon listening only on the IPv4 address is one that anybody who ran
.Dq dsconfigldap -a localhost
cannot reach, with nothing anywhere to say why.
.Pp
An address that cannot be bound is reported and skipped, so a machine with
IPv6 turned off still gets its IPv4 listener; only failing to bind every one
of them stops the daemon.
It must be an address and not a hostname - this setting says which socket to
open, which a name resolving to several things does not.
.Pp
The loopback, and it should stay that way.
.Xr ldapstns 8
has no transport security - see the StartTLS paragraph in
.Xr ldapstns 8 -
and its purpose is to be reached by
.Xr opendirectoryd 8
on the same machine.
.It Ic port No = Ar number
Default 389.
Binding it needs root, which is given up immediately afterwards, or launchd
socket activation, which avoids needing it at all.
.It Ic suffix No = Qq Ar dn
The naming context to serve.
Default
.Qq dc=stns .
.Pp
This is discovered rather than configured on the macOS side:
.Pa /System/Library/OpenDirectory/Mappings/RFC2307.plist
leaves the search base as
.Qq %! ,
which means
.Xr opendirectoryd 8
reads
.Sy namingContexts
from the root DSE, and this is the value it finds there.
It need not correspond to anything outside the machine.
Users are served below
.Ql cn=users
and groups below
.Ql cn=groups .
.It Ic user No = Qq Ar name
The unprivileged user to become after binding the socket.
Default
.Qq nobody .
.Pp
.Xr ldapstns 8
refuses to run as root, and verifies the change took effect rather than
assuming it.
Under launchd the question does not arise: the
.Sy Sockets
key in the job description has launchd bind the port before the job starts, so
the daemon can be started unprivileged and never holds root at all.
.It Ic interval No = Ar seconds
How often to re-read the directory from the API.
Default 60, minimum 10.
.Pp
A refresh happens between connections and never while a client is waiting, so
this trades freshness against load on the API server and nothing else.
If a refresh fails the previous directory is kept and served.
.It Ic bind_dn No = Qq Ar dn
.It Ic bind_password No = Qq Ar password
Require a client to bind as this DN with this password before it is answered.
Neither is set by default, and setting one without the other is an error.
.Pp
Without them the directory is open to any client that can reach the socket,
which on the loopback interface means every process on the machine.
That is usually the right trade: nothing served - names, ids, home directories,
shells, SSH public keys - is secret, and it is the difference between pointing
Directory Utility at the daemon in two fields and in four.
.Pp
With them, an anonymous client is refused with
.Sy insufficientAccessRights ,
and
.Xr opendirectoryd 8
has to be given the same credentials
.Pq Directory Utility, LDAPv3, Edit, "Use authentication when connecting" .
An anonymous bind still succeeds, because RFC 4513 says it must; it simply
authenticates nobody.
.It Ic expose_password No = Ar true | false
Serve the password hash as
.Sy userPassword .
Default false.
.Pp
.Xr ldapstns 8
refuses to start if this is set without a
.Ic bind_dn .
This is not a warning that can be ignored: a loopback socket is reachable by
every process on the machine, and serving crypt hashes to anonymous clients on
one would amount to a world-readable shadow file.
.Pp
Even with a
.Ic bind_dn
it is rarely the answer it looks like.
macOS cannot verify the SHA-512 hashes
.Pq Li $6$ Ns ...
that most STNS deployments store, so turning this on does not generally buy
working password authentication.
SSH keys, through
.Xr stns-key-wrapper 8 ,
are the path that works.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width /usr/local/etc/stns/client/stns.conf -compact
.It Pa /usr/local/etc/ldapstns.conf
The default location.
On Apple Silicon, where Homebrew installs into
.Pa /opt/homebrew ,
it is
.Pa /opt/homebrew/etc/ldapstns.conf .
.It Pa /usr/local/etc/stns/client/stns.conf
The STNS API client configuration.
.It Pa /usr/local/share/examples/ldapstns/ldapstns.conf
A commented sample.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
The whole file, for a machine whose
.Xr stns.conf 5
is already in place:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# nothing at all; the defaults serve dc=stns on 127.0.0.1:389
.Ed
.Pp
A directory only
.Xr opendirectoryd 8
may read, refreshed every five minutes:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
suffix        = "dc=example,dc=com"
interval      = 300
bind_dn       = "cn=reader,dc=example,dc=com"
bind_password = "correct horse battery staple"
.Ed
.Pp
Check it before installing it:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
ldapstns -n -f ./ldapstns.conf
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr stns.conf 5 ,
.Xr ldapstns 8 ,
.Xr stns-key-wrapper 8
